{
  "netStorageMeteringAcrossUpgrades": {
    "_info": {
      "comment": "What repeated writes to one storage slot cost, which is where net metering lives. Four independent slots in one body, each bracketed by its own gas reading: a single set, a set then a change (a dirty write), a set then a clear, and a write of zero over zero. AGHARTA IS A NON-EVENT HERE AND THAT IS AN ASSERTION. Ethereum adopted net metering at Constantinople and withdrew it at Petersburg; this chain bundled both into Agharta and so never ran it -- the production client's configuration carries the line for it commented out, beside a commented-out Petersburg. Every sequence therefore reads identically at Atlantis and Agharta, and anyone porting expectations from Ethereum's Constantinople gets this wrong. Net metering arrives at PHOENIX under EIP-2200. The rise at Magneto is cold-slot access being charged separately, not metering changing again.",
      "oracle": "core-geth production, evm t8n at each upgrade's rules, chain id 61",
      "oracle-version": "v1.12.21-unstable",
      "generated": "2026-08-23",
      "signing": "txbytes carries the signed transaction; the signing key is a filling input, not a fixture field, and the harness reads `sender`.",
      "eip": "EIP-2200 at Phoenix. EIP-1283 is NOT active on this chain at any height.",
      "slots": "0 to 3 hold the written values. 10 to 13 hold the gas each sequence consumed. 14 is the liveness marker.",
      "sequences": "10 = 0 to 1; 11 = 0 to 1 to 2; 12 = 0 to 1 to 0; 13 = 0 over 0",
      "unfilled-no-fork-name": "No expectation for ETC_Thanos: the generator's fork table has no name for them, so their rules cannot be selected. A tooling gap, not a claim that the case does not apply.",
      "unfilled-not-a-state-rule": "No expectation for ETC_DAOFork, ETC_DAOWars, ETC_FrontierThawing, ETC_MESSOff, ETC_MESSOn: these change no state-transition rule, so a state fixture has nothing to assert about them. Covering them needs block-level or chain-selection tests."
    },
